e could narrow the margin, though the district's ten-point Republican lean makes an outright upset unlikely.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Tom McClintock (R) is running."]}, "overview": "California's 5th District spans Sierra foothill and Central Valley communities and carries a Republican partisan lean of about ten points. Tom McClintock, the incumbent, advanced to the general election after the June primary and holds a large fundraising edge, with roughly $929,000 raised and $201,000 on hand. Democrat Michael Masuda, a challenger focused on small Central Valley towns, has raised about $362,000 with little outside help.\n\nNo public polls of the general election are available, so the outlook rests on district fundamentals. Every major rating, including Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, Sabato's Crystal Ball, and the Economist, places the seat at Safe or Solid Republican, and prediction markets give Masuda roughly a 7% chance. McClintock is favored to win another term, with the main question being the size of his margin rather than the outcome.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1154, "margin": -9.09}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1311, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0595, "margin": -11.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0545, "margin": -12.0}]}